{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "07", "section": "08", "chapter_title": "Productivity and Workflow Automation", "title": "Workflow Studio", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting workflow studio to published workflow site with revision trace, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the published workflow site with revision trace shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation", "cat100_supplement_workflow_studio", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 8, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/07/07"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_calibration"]}, "content": "# Workflow Studio\n\n![Workflow Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-08-workflow-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThis capstone section asks you to publish your workflow as a live web page, which means the abstract ideas from the rest of the chapter now have to survive contact with a real server. A page that looks fine on your laptop can return a 404, show a broken image, or fail to render a list once it is on the web, because publishing exposes three concrete requirements that local files hide: the homepage must be named exactly `index.html`, image paths must be relative (not pointing at your hard drive), and publishing must be turned on in the host's settings. This section teaches those mechanics using GitHub Pages.\n\nThe skill matters because \"it works on my machine\" is not the same as \"it works for everyone.\" A published artifact is the strongest evidence of digital fluency precisely because a stranger's browser, with none of your local context, has to render it correctly.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Name the homepage `index.html` so the site resolves at its root instead of returning a 404.\n- Use relative image paths so images load for every visitor, not just on your computer.\n- Enable publishing in the host's settings, since pushing files alone does not make a site live.\n- Close every HTML tag so the browser renders the content as intended.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student finishes a workflow write-up, pushes the files to GitHub, and visits the site URL, only to see a 404. The HTML is perfect, but the file is named `home.html`, and a web server looks for `index.html` at the root by default. They rename it, and the page appears, but the chart is a broken-image icon because the `<img>` tag points at `C:/Users/student/Desktop/chart.png`, a path that exists only on their laptop. No visitor's browser can reach the student's Desktop.\n\nThese are not coding mistakes in the usual sense; the markup is valid. They are publishing mistakes: the page assumed a local context that does not exist on the web.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPublishing a static site rests on three rules that local files let you ignore.\n\n**The homepage name.** A web server, asked for a site's root, looks for a file named `index.html`. Any other name (`home.html`, `main.html`) leaves the root with nothing to serve, producing a 404. This is a convention, not a choice; the file must be named `index.html`.\n\n**Relative versus absolute paths.** An absolute path like `C:/Users/you/chart.png` points to a specific location on your machine that no visitor can reach. A **relative path** like `./images/chart.png` points to a location relative to the HTML file itself, so it works wherever the site is hosted. The table makes the distinction concrete:\n\n| Path style | Example | Works for visitors? |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Absolute local | `C:/Users/you/chart.png` | No, points to your hard drive |\n| Absolute web | `https://site.com/img/chart.png` | Yes, but brittle if the site moves |\n| Relative | `./images/chart.png` | Yes, travels with the files |\n\n**Publishing must be enabled.** Pushing files to a repository stores them; it does not serve them. GitHub Pages requires you to set the source in Settings (branch and folder) before anything goes live. The files existing is necessary but not sufficient.\n\nUnderneath all three is one principle: a published page cannot rely on anything that lives only on your computer, including the file's local name, its disk paths, or your assumption that \"saved\" means \"served.\" Plus, well-formed HTML (every tag closed) is what lets the browser render structure like lists at all.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your page renders perfectly when you double-click the file on your laptop, with the chart image showing. After pushing to GitHub Pages, the live URL shows a broken-image icon. What is the most likely cause?\" explanation=\"Opening the file locally lets an absolute path like C:/Users/you/chart.png resolve, because your hard drive is right there. On the web no visitor can reach your Desktop, so the image breaks. The fix is a relative path such as ./images/chart.png that travels with the files. Local testing hides exactly this class of error.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The image uses an absolute local path that only resolves on your own machine\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"GitHub Pages cannot display PNG images\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The image file is too large to load over the web\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The browser needs the homepage renamed before any image will show\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is a real workflow write-up published as a GitHub Pages site.\n\n**The repo (note the homepage name and relative image path):**\n\n```\nmy-workflow-site/\n  index.html          <- must be index.html to serve at the site root\n  images/\n    audit-chart.png\n```\n\n**index.html:**\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head><title>My CAT 100 Workflow</title></head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>Weekly Workflow Audit</h1>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Log time per step</li>\n      <li>Find the slowest frequent step</li>\n      <li>Fix it, then measure again</li>\n    </ul>\n    <img src=\"./images/audit-chart.png\" alt=\"Before and after times\">\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\n**Publish:** in Settings > Pages, set Source = branch `main`, folder `/ (root)`.\n\nThree concrete decisions made it load:\n\n1. **Homepage named `index.html`** so the site root resolves instead of returning 404.\n2. **Relative image path `./images/audit-chart.png`** (not a `C:/Users/...` local path) so it works for every visitor.\n3. **Pages source set in Settings**, because pushing files alone does not enable publishing, and every `<li>` and `<ul>` is closed so the list renders.\n\nPutting a static site live follows a fixed order. Drag these five steps into the sequence that takes the page from a local folder to a working public URL.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the homepage index.html so the server resolves the site root instead of returning a 404\",\"Write asset references as relative paths like ./images/chart.png so they load for every visitor\",\"Confirm every HTML tag is closed so the browser renders lists and structure as intended\",\"Push the files to the repository, which stores them but does not yet serve them\",\"Enable the source in Settings then Pages (branch and folder) and open the live URL in a fresh browser to verify\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore declaring a site published, ask three things in order: is the homepage named `index.html`, do all asset paths point to locations a stranger's browser can reach (relative, not local), and is publishing actually turned on in the host's settings.\n\nA live site passes three checks:\n\n1. **Root check:** Is the homepage named `index.html`, so the site root resolves instead of 404?\n2. **Path check:** Do all images and assets use relative paths that load for any visitor?\n3. **Publish check:** Is the Pages source enabled in Settings, so the pushed files are actually served?\n\nIf any check fails, the files exist but the site does not work for anyone but you.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading is \"I pushed the files, so the site is live.\" Pushing stores the files; enabling Pages in Settings is what serves them. The two are separate steps.\n\nA second misreading is testing only by opening `index.html` from your hard drive, where local absolute image paths happen to work. Always test the published URL in a fresh browser, because that is the only context that reveals a broken path or a wrong filename.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the rules to add a second page, a resume, to the same site. You create `resume.html` alongside `index.html` and link to it from the homepage with `<a href=\"./resume.html\">Resume</a>`, a relative link so it works on the live site. The new page embeds a headshot with `<img src=\"./images/headshot.jpg\">`, again relative.\n\nYou push, then visit the live URL and click the link. If the resume 404s, the likely cause is a filename typo (`resume.htm` versus `resume.html`) or a missing push; if the headshot is broken, the path is wrong or the image was not committed. The walkthrough shows the same three rules governing every page you add: each must reach the others through relative links, each asset must use a relative path, and the whole thing only works once Pages is enabled and the files are actually pushed. Testing on the live URL, not the local file, is what catches the failures.\n\nDebug a failing deploy one symptom at a time, predicting the cause before it is revealed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Diagnose a GitHub Pages site that does not work\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You pushed your files and the root URL returns a 404. Your homepage file is named home.html. What is the fix?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Push the files a second time\",\"Rename home.html to index.html so the server can resolve the root\",\"Wait an hour for the server to find the file\",\"Add the filename to the end of the URL\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"The server looks for index.html at the root by default. Renaming home.html to index.html lets the root resolve instead of returning a 404.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The page now loads but the chart is a broken-image icon. The tag reads img src set to C:/Users/student/Desktop/chart.png. Why does it fail online?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The PNG format is unsupported on the web\",\"The path points to your hard drive, which no visitor can reach\",\"The image is committed but not pushed\",\"The alt text is missing\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"An absolute local path resolves only on your machine. Switch it to a relative path like ./images/chart.png so the image travels with the files and loads for every visitor.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You fixed both and re-pushed, but the live site still shows the old broken version. What did you most likely skip?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Nothing; the site will update on its own eventually\",\"Verify the change on the live URL in a fresh browser rather than trusting the local file\",\"Rename the repository\",\"Delete and recreate the images folder\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Opening the local file hides publishing errors because absolute paths resolve there. Always confirm on the public URL in a fresh browser, the only context with none of your local files.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Index-named homepage, relative asset paths, and verification on the live URL: the same three checks that make a deploy work are the ones that diagnose it when it breaks.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader never trusts a page until it has been opened from its public URL in a browser that has no access to their computer. \"It works locally\" is treated as untested, because local opening hides exactly the publishing errors that matter.\n\nA strong reader of a published site can answer four questions:\n\n1. Is the homepage named `index.html`, and does the root URL load it without a 404?\n2. Does every image and link use a path a stranger's browser can resolve?\n3. Is Pages actually enabled, and have the latest files been pushed?\n4. Does the page render correctly in a fresh browser, not just when opened from disk?\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_workflow_studio\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is the live site plus its repository structure. It is strong when a reader can open the public URL on a device that has never touched your files and see the page render fully, images and all, with a short note explaining the three publishing decisions.\n\nA useful note sounds like this: \"I renamed `home.html` to `index.html` so the root URL resolves, switched the chart from a `C:/Users/...` path to `./images/audit-chart.png` so it loads for visitors, and enabled Pages in Settings. I rejected linking the image from my Google Drive because a private-drive link would break for anyone not signed in as me.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove from GitHub Pages to a different host, say a university web space or Netlify. The three rules transfer: a default homepage filename (often still `index.html`), relative asset paths, and an explicit publish or deploy step. What adapts is the publishing mechanism: one host reads from a Git branch, another from an uploaded folder, another from a deploy command. Recognizing that the `index.html` and relative-path rules are universal while the \"turn on publishing\" step is host-specific is what lets you ship a site anywhere, not just on the one platform you learned.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the workflow studio packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-08-workflow-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"published GitHub Pages workflow site with relative paths\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the published GitHub Pages workflow site.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the index-and-relative-path rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the site.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the published workflow site with revision trace for CAT 100 07.08. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_workflow_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your site passes the root, path, and publish checks: the homepage is `index.html`, every asset uses a relative path, Pages is enabled, and the live URL renders correctly in a fresh browser.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_workflow_studio", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_workflow_studio", "cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"], "stem": "You are publishing your workflow write-up as a GitHub Pages site. What must the repo root homepage be named so the site URL works?", "options": ["home.html", "index.html", "main.html", "workflow.html"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "GitHub Pages serves index.html as the default page at the site root. Another name leaves the root URL showing a 404.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_wrong_index_name", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_workflow_studio", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_workflow_studio"], "stem": "Your live page shows a broken image. The tag reads <img src=\"C:/Users/me/diagram.png\">. What is the correct fix?", "options": ["Make the image bigger", "Use a repository-relative path like <img src=\"./images/diagram.png\"> and commit the image into the repo", "Switch the image to a PDF", "Add the image again to the same folder on your desktop"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A C:/Users path only resolves on your own machine. A relative path to a file inside the repo works for every visitor.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_absolute_local_path", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_workflow_studio", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_workflow_studio"], "stem": "You pushed index.html to the main branch but the site still does not load. What is the most likely missing step?", "options": ["The file needs to be renamed to readme.html", "In Settings > Pages, the source branch and folder were never selected, so Pages is not publishing", "You must push the file a second time", "GitHub does not host HTML"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Pushing files does not enable publishing on its own. Pages must have a source branch and folder set in Settings.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_no_pages_source_set", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_workflow_studio_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_workflow_studio", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_workflow_studio"], "stem": "Your list of workflow steps renders as one run-on line. The HTML is <ul><li>Log time<li>Find slow step<li>Fix it. What is wrong?", "options": ["Lists are not allowed in HTML", "The <li> items and the <ul> are not closed; each item needs </li> and the list needs </ul>", "You need to use a table instead", "The text is too short"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Unclosed and mis-nested tags break the layout. Closing each <li> and the <ul> renders a proper bulleted list.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_unclosed_tag", "difficulty": "hard"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "7fa6e2cf85f2817cb7c453613c0bec569bab932000eda3c02227c9a030521fbe", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_wrong_index_name", "pattern": "homepage_not_named_index", "description": "Publishing a GitHub Pages site whose homepage is not named index.html, so the root URL shows a 404", "trigger": "learner names the homepage home.html and the site root returns Not Found", "feedback": "Name the homepage index.html at the repo root (or /docs). GitHub Pages serves index.html as the default page.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_absolute_local_path", "pattern": "local_file_path_in_link", "description": "Linking to files with a local absolute path like C:/Users/... instead of a relative path inside the repo", "trigger": "an image tag points to C:/Users/me/pic.png and shows broken on the live site", "feedback": "Use repository-relative paths such as ./images/pic.png. A C:/Users path only works on your computer, not on the web.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_no_pages_source_set", "pattern": "pages_not_enabled", "description": "Pushing files but never selecting a Pages source branch and folder, so nothing is published", "trigger": "learner pushes to main but Settings > Pages source is still 'None'", "feedback": "In Settings > Pages, set the source to the branch (main) and folder (root or /docs). Pushing alone does not enable publishing.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_7_8_unclosed_tag", "pattern": "malformed_html_structure", "description": "Leaving an HTML tag unclosed or mis-nested so the page renders incorrectly", "trigger": "learner opens <ul> with list items but never writes </ul> and the layout breaks", "feedback": "Close every tag you open and keep nesting correct. Validate the markup so the page renders as intended.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}